meta {
  name: 04 Create speaking room
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/speaking/rooms
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{studentAccessToken}}
}

docs {
  This route CHANGED in Phase 5, and it is the one most likely to surprise a
  frontend developer.

  It used to be anonymous — rate-limited, but with no idea who was calling.
  A subscription cannot be enforced on a route that does not know the caller,
  so it now requires a token and a live subscription.

  The breaking-change risk turned out to be near zero: `GET
  /api/speaking/rooms/ice-servers` already required a token, and no WebRTC call
  completes without ICE servers. Any client that could finish a speaking
  session was already sending one.

  ## Checks

  - entitled student -> 201 with `{ roomId, hostToken, expiresAt }`
  - blocked student   -> 403 SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE
  - NO Authorization header -> 401 (this is the part that changed)

  ## The guest is still anonymous, on purpose

  Run `GET {{baseUrl}}/api/speaking/rooms/<roomId>` with NO token at all. It
  still returns 200.

  That is not a hole. A student practises with a partner who joins by link and
  may have no Lerniqo account — authenticating the socket or the lookup would
  break the feature rather than secure it. The entitlement is spent by the HOST
  when the room is created, which is what this request enforces.

  ## The one gap, documented rather than hidden

  A student blocked WHILE ALREADY IN a room keeps that room. Rooms are held in
  memory and deleted by a hard two-hour timer with no extension, so the window
  is at most two hours and cannot be renewed, and they cannot create another.
  That boundary is written down in `tasks/phases/05-subscription-access-plan.md`
  rather than left for someone to discover.
}
